//? Title: Average model predictions over a reference grid
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare methods at a common batch mix

Three methods were run under cool and warm conditions, with different
numbers of observations in each combination. A weighted linear model
allows each method to respond differently to the batch condition. The
reference grid asks how the methods compare if each is used in the same
mix of conditions, with 25% cool runs and 75% warm runs. The fit weights
describe the observations, and the grid weights describe the target mix.

```{cpp, id=marginal-means-and-contrasts, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/lm.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using datamunge::dstruct::DataFrame;
    using datamunge::linalg::DenseMatrix;
    using datamunge::stats::LM;
    using datamunge::stats::MarginalMeansOptions;

    DataFrame data, grid;
    data.add_column("method", std::vector<std::string>{
        "A", "A", "A", "A", "A", "B", "B", "B", "B", "B", "C", "C", "C", "C", "C"});
    data.add_column("batch", std::vector<std::string>{
        "Cool", "Cool", "Cool", "Warm", "Warm", "Cool", "Cool", "Warm",
        "Warm", "Warm", "Cool", "Cool", "Warm", "Warm", "Warm"});
    data.add_column("yield", std::vector<double>{8, 9, 10, 12, 13, 12, 14, 16, 17, 18, 13, 15, 16, 19, 19});
    data.add_column("precision", std::vector<double>{1, 2, 1, 1, 3, 2, 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2, 1, 3});
    const LM fit(data, "yield ~ method * batch", {.weights_column = "precision"});

    const std::vector<std::string> methods{"B", "A", "C", "B", "A", "C"};
    grid.add_column("method", methods);
    grid.add_column("batch", std::vector<std::string>{"Warm", "Warm", "Warm", "Cool", "Cool", "Cool"});
    const MarginalMeansOptions options{
        .groups = methods,
        .weights = {3, 3, 3, 1, 1, 1},
        .contrasts = DenseMatrix<double>(2, 3, {1, -1, 0, 0, -1, 1}),
    };
    const auto result = fit.estimated_marginal_means(grid, options);
    const auto print_row = [](const std::string& label, const auto& row) {
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | [{:.3f}, {:.3f}] |",
                     label, row.estimate, row.standard_error, row.lower, row.upper);
    };
    std::println("| Method | Marginal mean | Standard error | Simultaneous 95% interval |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < result.groups.size(); ++i)
        print_row(result.groups[i], result.means.estimates[i]);

    std::println("\n| Contrast | Difference | Standard error | Simultaneous 95% interval |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const std::array<std::string, 2> labels{"B − A", "C − A"};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < labels.size(); ++i)
        print_row(labels[i], result.contrasts.estimates[i]);
    std::println("\nThe covariance between the differences is {:.3f}.", result.contrasts.covariance(0, 1));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Method | Marginal mean | Standard error | Simultaneous 95% interval |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| B | 15.917 | 0.629 | [14.070, 17.763] |
| A | 11.813 | 0.619 | [9.996, 13.629] |
| C | 17.083 | 0.530 | [15.528, 18.639] |

| Contrast | Difference | Standard error | Simultaneous 95% interval |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| B − A | 4.104 | 0.883 | [1.733, 6.475] |
| C − A | 5.271 | 0.815 | [3.082, 7.460] |

The covariance between the differences is 0.383.
// result_end

The contrast columns follow the first appearance of group labels in the
grid. Both differences use method A, so their estimates share its
uncertainty. The reported intervals use the fitted residual degrees of
freedom and apply a Bonferroni adjustment to each table.

